CImg changes
============

How the vendored CImg is now maintained
---------------------------------------

inst/include/CImg.h is GENERATED, not stored in the repository. It is built at
build time from:

  tools/cimg/CImg_vanilla.h        upstream CImg, unmodified
  tools/cimg/patches/*.patch       one file per local fix

by tools/cimg/apply_patches.sh, which configure (Unix) and configure.win
(Windows) both call. The generated header is gitignored and excluded from the
source tarball, so it cannot go stale.

The authoritative inventory of local changes is therefore the patches/
directory, not this file. tools/check_cimg_patches.R is the inventory guard: it
checks that every expected patch is present, that each still contains the fix
it is supposed to carry, and that no unlisted patch has appeared. CI runs it
before the build.

The division of labour is deliberate. A patch that no longer applies makes
configure fail, so the build catches it without any help. What configure cannot
see is a patch that has been *deleted*: the rest of the set still applies, the
build stays green, and the fix is simply gone. That is the case the inventory
check exists for, and it is the case this project has actually been bitten by.

Why it is done this way: this file has been wrong twice, and the pre-patched
header lost fixes silently on re-vendor (the 2021 ss.back() fix and the 2025
vector() fix, years apart). With the patches as files, the difference from
upstream is always exactly the patch set, and a re-vendor is
"replace CImg_vanilla.h, re-run the check, port whatever fails".

Re-vendoring to a newer CImg
----------------------------

  1. Replace tools/cimg/CImg_vanilla.h with the new upstream header.
  2. Rscript tools/check_cimg_patches.R
  3. For each patch that no longer applies, port the fix to the new upstream
     code by hand and regenerate the patch (see apply_patches.sh for the
     command). If upstream fixed it, delete the patch file and remove its entry
     from check_cimg_patches.R.
  4. Rebuild. configure applies the patches before compiling.
  5. Add a section below recording what happened to each patch.

Changes are listed newest first.

10/11/2026  Patches split out of the vendored header
-----------------------------------------------------

The single pre-patched CImg.h was replaced by CImg_vanilla.h plus six patch
files, applied at build time. The generated header is byte-identical to the
previous committed one, so no behaviour changed; this is purely how the changes
are stored. The six patches, in application order:

  01-restore-dimension-string-assign.patch
  02-fix-vector-tainted-argument.patch
  03-guard-strellipsize-null-data.patch
  04-restore-resize-halfXY.patch
  05-guard-load-analyze-null-data.patch
  06-no-eof-to-stdout-in-r-mode.patch

The descriptions in the 29/10/2026 section below still apply to them.

29/10/2026  Re-vendored to CImg 4.0.7
-------------------------------------

Replaced the header with upstream CImg 4.0.7 (cimg_version 407), then re-applied
the patches that were still needed. This section is the record of what happened
to each patch carried on 3.3.4; it is derived from a diff of vanilla 3.3.4
against the previous vendored copy, not from the notes below.

Carried forward (not upstreamed, still required):

* vector() tainted-argument fix. The `!std::strncmp(ss,"vector",6)` guard tested
  `ss7<se1` and could taint its argument; it now tests
  `ss6<se1 && *ss6!='('`. Introduced in commit dcf8333 (2025-07-16), which had
  never been recorded here. Upstream 4.0.7 still has the old form.
* strellipsize null `str._data` guard in the math parser's print path
  (commit f174543). Upstream 4.0.7 identical.
* load_analyze null `body._data` guard (commit f174543). Upstream 4.0.7
  identical.
* save() must not write EOF to stdout in R mode (commit 7bd7e3e): the
  `std::fputc(EOF,cimg::_stdout())` call is wrapped in `#ifndef cimg_use_r`.
  Upstream 4.0.7 identical, and with `cimg_use_r` defined CImg's `_stdout()`
  throws, so this is load-bearing.

Superseded upstream, so no longer carried as patches:

* The gcc-14 sprintf buffer fix (`CImg<charT> format(8)` -> 15) in
  mp_v2s. 4.0.7 uses `(32)`, which is larger.
* The OpenMP removal from the six forward-warp paths (30/09/2026, below).
  4.0.7 is also serial in those branches, and additionally accumulates with
  `is_added` and normalises by an `accu` count. That is strictly better: see
  the correctness note below.

Dropped, not required:

* The openexr `#pragma GCC diagnostic` commenting-out. imager never defines
  `cimg_use_openexr` (checked configure.ac and all three Makevars), so the
  block is not compiled.

Already lost before this re-vendor, and left lost:

* The 2021 `ss.back() = 0` -> `if (ss.back() != 0)` fix (08/06/2021, below).
  The 2023 re-vendor to 3.3.4 silently dropped it; eight bare `ss.back() = 0`
  remain. Not restored, since the surrounding code has changed in 4.x and the
  original fault has not been re-demonstrated. Recorded here so the loss is
  visible rather than forgotten again.

Newly added in this re-vendor (API the imager R interface depends on, which
upstream removed between 3.3.4 and 4.0.x):

* CImg::resize_halfXY() / get_resize_halfXY(). Upstream removed it between
  3.3.4 and 4.0.0, but imager::resize_halfXY() wraps it and
  imager::resize_uniform() exposes it to R. Body is unchanged from 3.3.4,
  including the 9-tap kernel.
* The dimension-string family: assign(const CImg<t>&, const char*const) and
  assign(const CImg<t>&, const char*const, const T&). Upstream removed these
  in 4.0.x while leaving the two public constructors that call them, which is
  worse than it sounds: `CImg<double> out(other,"xyzc",0.0)` no longer finds the
  string overload, so the `const char*` decays to `bool` and binds
  `assign(img, is_shared=true)`. That throws "pixel types are different" for a
  cross-type copy and would silently alias buffers for a same-type one.
  Sixteen call sites in imager hit this (reductions, filtering, hough, drawing,
  utils).

Forward warps are now CORRECT, not merely deterministic. This is the important
change and it fixes a real bug that serialising could not. A forward warp
scatters source pixels into the output; the old code used set_linear_atXY()
without `is_added`, i.e. an iterated blend

    dest = w1*value + (1-w1)*dest

At an integer destination dx=dy=0, so w1=1 and w2=0, and each scatter performs
`dest = value`: last write wins. A contracting forward warp (the decimation case
that matters for image stacking) therefore kept one arbitrary source pixel per
destination instead of averaging its contributors. Measured on a 40x40 noise
image with an 8x8-block collapse: the output carried the sd of a single pixel
(1.0) where a mean of 64 should give 0.125, and the populated cell held exactly
the last-scanned source value. 4.0.7 accumulates with `is_added=true` into `res`
plus a count into `accu`, then divides at the end, which is order-independent,
correct, and a commutative reduction.

Guarded by "forward warps average their contributions" in
tests/testthat/test_warp_determinism.R, which fails on the old behaviour and
needs no OpenMP.

30/09/2026
----------

* CImg::get_warp(): removed the OpenMP "parallel for" pragma from the six
  forward-warp paths (mode 2 and mode 3, for 1D/2D/3D warping fields, with
  linear or higher interpolation).

  A forward warp scatters source pixels into the result through
  set_linear_atX{Y,Z}(), which accumulates with an iterated read-modify-write
  blend:

      dest = (T)(w1*value + w2*dest)

  When several source pixels map to the same destination, threads running the
  loop concurrently read and write that same cell, so the accumulated value
  depends on the order in which the scatters happen to interleave. The result
  was therefore non-deterministic and, for maps that contract, visibly wrong:
  on a 200x150 test image roughly 2300 of 30000 pixels differed from the
  serial result, with an average difference of about 0.27 (compared with pixel
  values of order 1). Atomic updates would not fix this, because the operation
  is not a commutative reduction - the order of the blends changes the answer -
  so only a fixed scatter order is correct.

  SUPERSEDED 29/10/2026 by the 4.0.7 re-vendor, which fixes the root cause
  (accumulate + normalise) rather than serialising the broken blend, and also
  fixes the last-write-wins error described above. The old text claimed
  "upstream CImg 4.06 still contains the same bug"; that was wrong, and 4.0.6 is
  where upstream introduced the accumulator.

  The backward-warp paths (mode 0 and mode 1) were left parallelised: they
  gather into disjoint output cells and are unaffected.

